    <h1>How to Play Murder Sudoku: Rules, Clues, and a Solved Case</h1>

    <p>Murder sudoku is what happens when a sudoku grid witnesses a crime. You get a floor plan carved into rooms, a set of named suspects, one very still victim, and a handful of witness statements. Your job is to work out where everyone was standing, using pure deduction, and the person who turns out to be alone in the room with the victim is the killer. No trivia, no dice, no guessing: every case has exactly one solution, and you can prove it.</p>

    <p>This guide covers the complete rules, the five types of witness statements you will meet, and a fully solved 4×4 case that walks through every deduction step by step. Five minutes from now, you will know how to catch a cat culprit yourself.</p>

    <h2>What Is Murder Sudoku?</h2>

    <p>Murder sudoku belongs to the logic grid puzzle family, but it swaps numbers for suspects and boxes for rooms. The genre exploded out of print: a wave of murder mystery puzzle books topped bestseller lists and sold well over a million copies, with series like <em>Murdoku</em> leading the charge. The online versions translate the same idea to the browser, where the grid handles the bookkeeping for you.</p>

    <p>What makes it different from every other grid puzzle is the payoff. In Sudoku you finish a grid, in Shikaku you finish a division, and in murder sudoku you finish an alibi. The final deduction names a culprit, which gives every placement a small narrative weight: you are not just filling cells, you are building the timeline of a crime.</p>

    <div class="cta-box">
      <p>Love pure deduction? <a href="/">Play Shikaku free →</a> &nbsp;&middot;&nbsp; <a href="/akari/">Play Akari free →</a></p>
    </div>

    <h2>The Board: Rooms, Walls, and Objects</h2>

    <p>Every case starts on a square grid, from a compact 4×4 beginner case up to a sprawling 10×10 investigation. The grid is not one open floor plan: it is carved into irregular rooms with names like Kitchen, Library, and Bedroom. Almost every witness statement refers back to a room, so your first move in any case is the same: learn the floor plan.</p>

    <p>Some cells hold objects, and they come in three flavors:</p>

    <ul>
      <li><strong>Blocking objects</strong> (a table, a bookshelf, a piano) fill their cell completely. Nobody can stand there, which quietly shrinks everyone's options.</li>
      <li><strong>Floor objects</strong> (a rug, a carpet) are decoration. Suspects can stand on them freely, and clue statements like "on a carpet" point straight at them.</li>
      <li><strong>Furniture</strong> (a chair, a stool) sits on top of a floor object, and a suspect can occupy that cell too. One square can hold a rug, a chair, and a nervous suspect all at once.</li>
    </ul>

    <h2>The Suspects and the Sudoku Rule</h2>

    <p>Each case has a cast of suspects labeled A, B, C and onward, plus one additional figure: <strong>V, the victim</strong>. Every figure stands on exactly one cell, and here is the sudoku half of the bargain:</p>

    <p><strong>No two figures, victim included, ever share a row or a column.</strong></p>

    <p>That single rule does an enormous amount of work. Place one suspect and you instantly eliminate their entire row and column for everyone else. On a 4×4 grid with four figures, it means each row and each column holds exactly one person, which turns the board into a puzzle about positions rather than numbers.</p>

    <h2>How You Find the Killer</h2>

    <p>The victim was not alone when it happened. The governing rule of every case is this: <strong>the victim shares their room with exactly one other suspect, and that suspect is the killer.</strong></p>

    <p>Every other suspect can share a room with as many people as the clues allow. Only the victim's room is restricted. So the endgame of every case is the same satisfying moment: you finish placing everyone, look at the victim's room, and there is exactly one name standing next to the body. Case closed.</p>

    <h2>Witness Statements: The Five Clue Types</h2>

    <p>Every suspect except the victim comes with one clue, written as a plain witness statement. They fall into five broad types:</p>

    <table>
      <tr><th>Type</th><th>Example statements</th><th>What it does</th></tr>
      <tr><td>Location</td><td>"He was in the Kitchen." / "She was NOT in the Gallery."</td><td>Pins a suspect inside one room, or crosses a whole room out for them.</td></tr>
      <tr><td>Objects</td><td>"He was on a carpet."</td><td>Narrows a suspect to cells with a floor object.</td></tr>
      <tr><td>Architecture</td><td>"She was beside a table." / "He was at a wall." / "She was in a corner."</td><td>Describes where the suspect stood relative to the room's furniture or walls.</td></tr>
      <tr><td>Social</td><td>"He was with Diana in the Library." / "She was alone with Boris."</td><td>Ties two suspects to the same room, or demands a room held exactly two people.</td></tr>
      <tr><td>Case-wide</td><td>"Exactly 3 people were in the Ballroom." / "The Cellar was locked, nobody inside."</td><td>Rules that govern a whole room regardless of who you are reasoning about.</td></tr>
    </table>

    <p>The social clues are usually the most powerful, because anything true of one suspect instantly constrains the other. The case-wide rules are easy to forget and costly to overlook, so read every statement once before you place anyone.</p>

    <AdSlot />

    <h2>A Solved 4×4 Case: Who Ambushed Old Whiskers?</h2>

    <p>Time to catch a culprit. A small house, three cat suspects, and one victim. Old Whiskers was found in the Kitchen, and the three cats each leave exactly one witness statement:</p>

    <ul>
      <li><strong>Mochi (M):</strong> "I was in the Kitchen."</li>
      <li><strong>Tofu (T):</strong> "I was in the Library."</li>
      <li><strong>Pixel (P):</strong> "I was on the rug."</li>
    </ul>

    <p>Here is the floor plan. The victim V is already in place in the Kitchen, and the rug in the Bedroom is marked with a dotted texture:</p>

    <div class="case-board" role="img" aria-label="A 4 by 4 case grid. The Kitchen covers the top-left two by two cells and holds the victim V. The Library is L-shaped along the top right. The Bedroom fills the rest. A rug occupies the bedroom cell in the bottom row, third column.">
      <div class="case-cell room-kitchen"><span class="room-label">Kitchen</span></div>
      <div class="case-cell room-kitchen cell-victim">V</div>
      <div class="case-cell room-library"><span class="room-label">Library</span></div>
      <div class="case-cell room-library"></div>
      <div class="case-cell room-kitchen"></div>
      <div class="case-cell room-kitchen"></div>
      <div class="case-cell room-library"></div>
      <div class="case-cell room-library"></div>
