    <h2>Three Habits of a Good Detective</h2>

    <ul>
      <li><strong>Read every statement before placing anyone.</strong> Case-wide rules like "exactly 3 people in the Ballroom" apply to suspects you have not reasoned about yet, and missing one is the most common way to paint yourself into a corner.</li>
      <li><strong>Start with the most constrained suspect.</strong> A statement that pins a suspect to a small room next to the victim's row and column can collapse to a single cell immediately. Forced placements cascade, one locked suspect quietly deletes a row and a column for everyone else.</li>
      <li><strong>Trust the uniqueness guarantee.</strong> A fair case has exactly one solution, so if two placements both seem to work, you have misread a statement. Re-read the witness statements, especially the words "NOT", "alone", and "exactly", before you doubt the puzzle.</li>
    </ul>

    <h2>Frequently Asked Questions</h2>

    <details><summary>What is murder sudoku?</summary><p>Murder sudoku is a hybrid of sudoku and a whodunit mystery. You place named suspects on a grid carved into rooms, guided by witness statements, while a sudoku rule keeps any two figures out of the same row or column. The suspect who ends up alone in the room with the victim is the killer.</p></details>

    <details><summary>Is murder sudoku the same as Murdoku?</summary><p>Not exactly. Murdoku is a popular puzzle-book series that helped drive the murder sudoku trend, similar to how KenKen is one brand of calcudoku. Murder sudoku is the general puzzle type, and several sites and book series publish their own versions of it.</p></details>

    <details><summary>Do the clues in a murder sudoku ever contradict each other?</summary><p>No. A well-made case is guaranteed to have exactly one arrangement that satisfies every witness statement, every room rule, and the sudoku row and column rule. If your grid stops working, an earlier placement needs to be reconsidered, not the case itself.</p></details>

